-- title:
--   Compatible finitely generated ideals cut out a finitely presented closed subscheme
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a scheme, and suppose given an assignment $I$ which to every commutative ring $R$ (a type in the ambient universe) and every morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} R \to E$ attaches an ideal $I(R,s) \subseteq R$. Two hypotheses are imposed. First, compatibility with base change: for all commutative rings $R, R'$, every ring homomorphism $\psi : R \to R'$ and every $s : \operatorname{Spec} R \to E$, one has $I(R', \operatorname{Spec}(\psi) \text{ followed by } s) = \psi_*(I(R,s))$, the ideal of $R'$ generated by the image of $I(R,s)$ under $\psi$. Second, finite generation: $I(R,s)$ is a finitely generated ideal for every $R$ and every $s$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a scheme $Z$ and a morphism $\iota : Z \to E$ such that $\iota$ is a closed immersion, $\iota$ is locally of finite presentation, and for every commutative ring $R$ and every $s : \operatorname{Spec} R \to E$ the morphism $s$ factors through $\iota$ — that is, there exists $z : \operatorname{Spec} R \to Z$ with $z$ followed by $\iota$ equal to $s$ — if and only if $I(R,s)$ is the zero ideal.
--
--   This is the representability statement for a closed subfunctor of a scheme cut out by a base-change compatible family of ideals on affine points, in the refined form in which finite generation of the ideals yields a closed immersion that is locally of finite presentation. It is the tool used to produce the closed subschemes of moduli-type parameter schemes occurring in the treatment of polarised abelian schemes, where conditions such as being theta-adapted, the existence of a level generator, or the canonical polarisation data conditions are shown to be closed and of finite presentation.
